</style>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/4.7.0/css/font-awesome.min.css">
</head>
<body class="article toc2 toc-right">
<div id="header">
<h1>Layers and localization</h1>
<div id="toc" class="toc2">
<div id="toctitle">Table of Contents</div>
<ul class="sectlevel1">
<li><a href="#project_layering">Project layering</a></li>
<li><a href="#creating_a_layer">Creating a layer</a></li>
<li><a href="#layer_configuration">Layer configuration</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#roles_and_access">Roles and access</a></li>
<li><a href="#applications">Applications</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#content_inheritance">Content inheritance</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#propagation">Propagation</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#working_in_a_layer">Working in a layer</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#navigator_indicators">Navigator indicators</a></li>
<li><a href="#layers_panel">Layers context panel</a></li>
<li><a href="#localize">Edit and Localize</a></li>
<li><a href="#ai_translation">AI translation</a></li>
<li><a href="#rename_move_and_sort">Rename, move, and sort</a></li>
<li><a href="#reset_inheritance">Reset inheritance</a></li>
<li><a href="#publishing_and_versions">Publishing and versions</a></li>
<li><a href="#archive_and_delete">Archive and delete</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#understanding_the_cms_model">Understanding the CMS model</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="content">
<div id="preamble">
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Content layers let editors reuse or adapt content across languages, regions, brands, or channels while changing only what each destination needs.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="project_layering"><a class="link" href="#project_layering">Project layering</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>A <strong>layer</strong> is a <a href="../projects.ahtml">content project</a> that has a parent project. Like any other project, it has its own repository, default language, permissions, editorial workflow, and published content. The difference is that it inherits content from the parent project. The project at the top of a layer tree is the <strong>root project</strong>.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Layers can be nested or arranged as siblings. For example, a global project can have Norwegian and German child layers, while the German layer can have separate Austrian and Swiss descendants. Content and changes can therefore flow through several generations of layers.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-overview.png" alt="Content Studio Settings showing a root project with several child layers, including one with two child layers of its own" width="1430px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 1. Layers overview presented as a directed graph with a root project and several child layers</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="creating_a_layer"><a class="link" href="#creating_a_layer">Creating a layer</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Create a layer with the <a href="manage.ahtml#project-wizard">project wizard</a>. On the first step, select the project that the new layer should inherit from, then choose the layer&#8217;s default language. Use the <strong>Copy from&#8230;&#8203;</strong> option to use the same language as the parent.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-step1.png" alt="Project wizard with a parent project and Danish as the default language" width="905px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 2. Select the parent project and default language</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Name the layer and review its generated identifier. Keeping the identifier similar to the parent&#8217;s makes the relationship easier to recognize. For example, a Swedish layer below <code>hmdb</code> could use <code>hmdb-se</code>.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-step2.png" alt="Project wizard with the new layer&#8217;s display name and identifier" width="905px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 3. Name the layer and set its identifier</div>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock important">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-important" title="Important"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
The parent project and project identifier cannot be changed after the layer is created. The default language and other project settings can be changed later.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Choose the layer&#8217;s access mode. Use the <strong>Copy from&#8230;&#8203;</strong> option to apply the same default content visibility as the parent.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-step3.png" alt="Project wizard showing the access mode choices for the new layer" width="848px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 4. Choose the default content visibility</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Complete the remaining project wizard steps to assign roles and review the applications inherited from the parent. When the project is created, a background synchronization job starts copying the parent&#8217;s content into the new layer.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="layer_configuration"><a class="link" href="#layer_configuration">Layer configuration</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="roles_and_access"><a class="link" href="#roles_and_access">Roles and access</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Each layer has its own project roles and content permissions, allowing access to be controlled independently for every layer.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Access to a parent project does not automatically grant access to its child layers, or vice versa. Assign users the appropriate <a href="../projects.ahtml#project-roles">project role</a> in every layer where they work.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
Users with the <strong>Content Manager Administrator</strong> or <strong>System Administrator</strong> role can access all layers by default.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="applications"><a class="link" href="#applications">Applications</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>A layer inherits the <a href="../projects.ahtml#project-applications">project applications</a> assigned to its parent. Inherited applications cannot be removed from the child layer, but additional applications can be assigned there. Application inheritance follows the project hierarchy independently of whether individual content items have been localized.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="content_inheritance"><a class="link" href="#content_inheritance">Content inheritance</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>An inherited item keeps the same content ID in every layer. Content Studio can therefore identify corresponding items across the layer tree even when their names, locations, or editorial data differ.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
A layer can also contain local content created directly in that layer. These items are not visible in parent projects, but are inherited by descendants of the layer where they were created.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Inheritance is tracked independently for four dimensions:</p>
</div>
<table class="tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 20%;">
<col style="width: 40%;">
<col style="width: 40%;">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">Dimension</th>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">What is inherited</th>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">What makes it local</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock"><strong>Content</strong></p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Editorial data, including values entered in the content form.</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Localizing the item in the layer.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock"><strong>Name</strong></p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">The path name of the corresponding parent item.</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Renaming the item in the layer.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock"><strong>Parent</strong></p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">The item&#8217;s location beneath its parent in the content tree.</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Moving the item in the layer.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock"><strong>Sort</strong></p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">The default ordering of the item&#8217;s children.</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Changing the sort order in the layer.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Content Studio updates the relevant inheritance dimension automatically when an editor localizes, renames, moves, or sorts an item. Changing one dimension does not stop the others from inheriting. For example, an item can have a local name and location while its content form continues to receive changes from the parent layer.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
In Content Studio, an item described simply as <strong>localized</strong> normally has localized <strong>Content</strong> data. It may still inherit its name, parent, or sort order.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="propagation"><a class="link" href="#propagation">Propagation</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Parent changes are automatically replicated to descendants for each dimension that they still inherit. If one dimension is local, the remaining dimensions continue receiving updates.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Consider three nested layers, <strong>A</strong> → <strong>B</strong> → <strong>C</strong>:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p>If <strong>B</strong> localizes only the content data, later content changes from <strong>A</strong> stop at <strong>B</strong>. Name, parent, and sort changes from <strong>A</strong> can still propagate through <strong>B</strong> to <strong>C</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The localized content from <strong>B</strong> becomes the inherited source for <strong>C</strong>, unless <strong>C</strong> has localized its own content.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Content created locally in <strong>B</strong> is inherited by <strong>C</strong>, but does not appear in <strong>A</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>New items and their changes initially propagate to child layers. After an item is marked <strong>Ready</strong> in the parent layer for the first time, only subsequent versions marked <strong>Ready</strong> propagate. This keeps unfinished parent edits out of established downstream workflows and reduces unnecessary versions. See <a href="../workflow.ahtml">Workflow</a> for the available workflow states.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Replication normally runs in the background immediately after a change. A consistency job also reconciles the layers if initial synchronization or an individual update is interrupted.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="working_in_a_layer"><a class="link" href="#working_in_a_layer">Working in a layer</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Editors create, organize, and publish content in a layer much as they do in any other project. Actions are performed on the item in the current layer; an item is never moved between layers for editing.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="navigator_indicators"><a class="link" href="#navigator_indicators">Navigator indicators</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Items whose content data is still inherited are visually subdued in the <a href="../workbench/navigator.ahtml">Navigator</a>. A Layers indicator appears beside the publishing status of inherited and localized items. When the <a href="../contextpanel/layers.ahtml">Layers panel</a> is available, selecting the indicator opens the panel for that item.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-navigator.png" alt="Navigator showing Layers indicators beside inherited content items" width="634px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 5. A small layer icon is displayed next to the publish status in the content list</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="layers_panel"><a class="link" href="#layers_panel">Layers context panel</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <a href="../contextpanel/layers.ahtml">Layers context panel</a> shows the corresponding item throughout the layer tree, including its publishing and localization status in each project. Use it to inspect the item&#8217;s state, edit it in the current layer, or open it in another accessible layer.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="localize"><a class="link" href="#localize">Edit and Localize</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Inherited content data is read-only. Opening an inherited item in the Content Editor displays the form in a disabled state. Click <b class="button">Localize</b> to stop inheriting its content data and enable editing in the current layer.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-disabled.png" alt="Content Editor with a disabled form and the Localize action available for an inherited item" width="546px">
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Localizing the item:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p>stops <strong>Content</strong> inheritance from the parent;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>assigns the layer&#8217;s default language;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>enables the item for editing in the <a href="../editor.ahtml">Content Editor</a>; and</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>leaves name, parent, and sort inheritance unchanged.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The localized draft can be edited and published independently of the corresponding item in every other layer.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Also notice that the editor toolbar presents the inheritance icon together with the content name, giving you a visual indication of inheritance status.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-edit-icon.png" alt="Editor toolbar showing the inheritance icon to the left of the content name and workflow status" width="326px">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 6. Instantly see the inheritance status of an item in the editor toolbar</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Clicking the icon will open the <a href="../contextpanel/layers.ahtml">Layers panel</a> if it is available.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="ai_translation"><a class="link" href="#ai_translation">AI translation</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>When enabled, the <a href="../ai.ahtml#ai_translator">AI Translator</a> opens after an item is localized and can translate supported text into the current layer&#8217;s language. Review the translated values in the Content Editor before saving or publishing them.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="rename_move_and_sort"><a class="link" href="#rename_move_and_sort">Rename, move, and sort</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Renaming, moving, and sorting inherited content do not localize its content data. Each action makes only its corresponding inheritance dimension local:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p><a href="../actions/move.ahtml">Move</a> changes <strong>Parent</strong> inheritance when the item is moved to another parent.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Renaming the path in the <a href="../editor/content-form.ahtml">Content Form</a> changes <strong>Name</strong> inheritance.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><a href="../actions/sort.ahtml">Sort</a> changes <strong>Sort</strong> inheritance for the parent whose children are reordered.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The content form continues to inherit parent changes until the item is explicitly localized.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="reset_inheritance"><a class="link" href="#reset_inheritance">Reset inheritance</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Once an item&#8217;s content data has been localized, <b class="button">Reset</b> is available in the Content Editor. Reset restores inheritance for every dimension that was made local, refreshes those values from the parent, and returns the content data to a read-only state.</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/layering-reset.png" alt="Localized content item with the Reset action available in the Content Editor" width="546px">
</div>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock important">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-important" title="Important"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
Reset replaces local content data, name, location, and sort order with the current values from the parent. Earlier local versions remain available in <a href="../contextpanel/version-history.ahtml">Version history</a>, but the reset action itself cannot be undone directly.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="publishing_and_versions"><a class="link" href="#publishing_and_versions">Publishing and versions</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Every layer has its own <em>draft</em> and <em>master</em> branches. Inheritance updates the layer&#8217;s draft, but does not publish the item there. Each layer can review, schedule, publish, and unpublish its corresponding item independently.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Version history is also scoped to the current layer. The panel shows versions and activities recorded in that layer without mixing in timelines from its parent, siblings, or descendants.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="archive_and_delete"><a class="link" href="#archive_and_delete">Archive and delete</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Archiving an item moves that item and its descendants into the current layer&#8217;s <a href="../workbench/archive.ahtml">Archive</a> and takes any published versions offline. If the item was inherited, its archived copy becomes local and stops receiving inherited changes. The corresponding item in the parent layer is not affected.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>When an item is archived in a parent layer, the action propagates to descendants that still inherit both its <strong>Content</strong> and <strong>Parent</strong> dimensions. A descendant remains active if either dimension is local or if inbound references from items outside the archived selection prevent it from being archived.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Restoring an item from a layer&#8217;s Archive restores that layer&#8217;s archived copy as local content. If an inherited item is permanently deleted while its origin still exists in the parent, synchronization can create a fresh inherited copy in the layer. Permanent deletion is therefore not a way to exclude parent content from a layer; leave the item archived instead.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>See <a href="../actions/delete.ahtml#delete_in_layers">Delete in layers</a> for the complete Delete workflow.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="understanding_the_cms_model"><a class="link" href="#understanding_the_cms_model">Understanding the CMS model</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>For details about the underlying properties and replication engine, see the <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/cms/stable/content/layering">Content layers reference</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
